Tind AI box
===========

Super+K opens the chat window (tind-ai-ask). The pieces, and who can do what:

  human            chats in the window. Typed messages go straight out (Direct) after the fixed rules have hidden names,
                   addresses, keys and secrets; each message shows what was hidden. Attaching system data (system log,
                   Tind report, Steam logs, files) makes a message Checked: the exact text is shown before it is sent.
                   "Unredacted" sends one message exactly as typed. Nothing in an answer runs by itself. Chats are kept
                   in ~/.local/share/tind-ai/chats/<provider>/ (0600) until deleted in the window.
  tind-ai-local    system user, never logs in. Its service (tind-ai-local.socket/.service) adds the system journal
                   when asked and redacts everything into a briefing. No network at all, no /home, writes only
                   /var/lib/tind-ai/briefing/<uid> and /var/lib/tind-ai/local (placeholder maps).
  tind-ai-remote   system user, never logs in. One service per provider (tind-ai-remote@PROVIDER), each with only its
                   own API key, reads only the briefings, writes only /var/lib/tind-ai/outbox/<uid>, and reaches only
                   the addresses /usr/lib/tind-ai/netallow resolved for its provider's API host.

Folders: briefing/<uid> is readable by that human and tind-ai-remote; outbox/<uid> by that human only; local/ by
tind-ai-local only. API keys: /etc/tind-ai/keys/PROVIDER (root only, handed to that provider's service as a systemd
credential), set from the Ask window through pkexec (/usr/lib/tind-ai/setkey).

Firewall: nftables table "inet tind_ai" (/usr/lib/tind-ai/firewall.nft, loaded by tind-ai-firewall.service). It is
separate from ufw and only ever drops traffic of the two AI users.

Local models (later): "This computer" in the provider list is served by tind-ai-local, so a model on this
computer runs with no network at all. It is grayed out until a local model is installed and the hardware allows it.

Providers: /usr/share/tind-ai/providers.json (Scaleway is the default; Anthropic and OpenAI are marked as outside the
EU). The custom endpoint (any OpenAI-compatible server; plain http only to 127.0.0.1) is kept in /etc/tind-ai/custom.json.

Prices: /usr/share/tind-ai/prices.json (tind-ai-ui), per million tokens in the provider's currency, shipped with
Tind updates and never fetched. A coloured mark by each model and the Prices page (window menu) show the band: blue
free (this computer), green cheap, amber mid, red expensive, gray unknown (a custom server). The band uses 3 parts
input to 1 part output, with the limits 1 and 10 from the same file.

Export (window menu, or the + menu): the Tind report, the system log and the Steam and Proton logs written to
~/AI-export (or a folder you pick) for AI apps outside the window, such as Claude Desktop. Those apps run as you,
outside the walls, so the local service applies the fixed rules first unless Unredacted is ticked; nothing is saved
in the briefing folder. Each export replaces the previous set; about.txt says when it was made and whether it is
redacted. The folder is 0700, the files 0600.

Check from a terminal: tind-ai-ask --check, tind-ai-ask --prices
